-> Variables hold a domain (a set of possible values), not a single slot
-> you overwrite. Constraints narrow that domain as you add them.
x ∈ Z
x > 10
x < 12

-> Three clues were enough to pin x to a single integer — nobody ever
-> wrote `x = 11`. This is real narrowing: the domain {integers} ∩ (10, 12)
-> has exactly one member.
assert x = 11

-> `=` is just a constraint too — narrowing a domain down to exactly one
-> value. It behaves identically whether you reach one value in a single
-> step or several.
y = 5
assert y = 5
